WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.840 --> 00:00:03.420
This is a seed.

00:00:04.160 --> 00:00:07.740
It doesn't look like much
right now, but if you ...

00:00:08.120 --> 00:00:09.200
put it in the ground,

00:00:11.460 --> 00:00:13.640
give it some water,

00:00:14.540 --> 00:00:16.280
fight off invaders,

00:00:17.180 --> 00:00:18.880
and wait a little while...

00:00:20.680 --> 00:00:24.420
After a few weeks with a little luck,

00:00:25.220 --> 00:00:27.940
you might end up with a head of lettuce.

00:00:29.080 --> 00:00:31.680
That's a lot of work for a hundred calories.

00:00:33.540 --> 00:00:36.600
But what if we had an easier way of doing things...

00:00:36.980 --> 00:00:39.460
One that didn’t require any soil,

00:00:39.460 --> 00:00:41.000
used much less water,

00:00:41.520 --> 00:00:43.400
and grew faster?

00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:49.440
One where food could actually grow itself?

00:00:55.320 --> 00:01:01.660
By 2050, the world's population is expected
to reach almost 10 billion people.

00:01:01.980 --> 00:01:04.200
That's a lot more mouths to feed.

00:01:05.080 --> 00:01:09.500
But, only 11 percent of land is even suitable for agriculture.

00:01:09.500 --> 00:01:11.980
So, farmers will have to turn to innovative

00:01:11.980 --> 00:01:14.840
and more efficient farming practices for a
solution.

00:01:16.520 --> 00:01:21.760
One of the most promising is a method called aquaponics,

00:01:21.760 --> 00:01:23.900
which doesn't require any soil,

00:01:23.920 --> 00:01:28.520
and involves raising fish and
plants together in a contained system.

00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:32.360
There are several different setups you
can use,

00:01:32.360 --> 00:01:34.760
but here's how the most popular method works.

00:01:35.780 --> 00:01:38.460
The process begins at the
fish rearing tank.

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:44.500
Here, highly adaptable fish like tilapia, are given plenty of fish food,

00:01:44.500 --> 00:01:48.740
which also leads to plenty of waste.

00:01:49.900 --> 00:01:53.700
The solid waste is filtered out by
flowing water,

00:01:54.980 --> 00:02:01.960
but the water—which is still full of ammonia from the fish excrement—is mixed with special plastic pieces.

00:02:02.340 --> 00:02:06.200
Naturally occurring bacteria grow
on these surfaces,

00:02:06.200 --> 00:02:13.380
which helps convert the toxic ammonium in
the water into nitrites and then nitrates.

00:02:14.220 --> 00:02:16.599
Nitrates are basically plant food.

00:02:17.160 --> 00:02:21.640
So, the nutrient-rich water flows out into the growing area,

00:02:21.660 --> 00:02:27.020
where seedlings on floating rafts can absorb the food directly through their roots.

00:02:27.160 --> 00:02:29.120
No soil required.

00:02:29.580 --> 00:02:35.900
Above them, energy-efficient LED lights are adjusted to provide the optimal color spectrum.

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:43.240
All of this comes together, helping the plants grow up to twice as fast as land crops.

00:02:43.960 --> 00:02:50.500
And because the plant roots purify the water, 
the cycle can start all over again.

00:02:54.980 --> 00:03:01.020
Commercial systems can yield up to 12
times as much produce per square foot

00:03:01.020 --> 00:03:03.140
as traditional farming methods.

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:07.740
Leafy greens like lettuce are the easiest crops to grow,

00:03:07.740 --> 00:03:12.520
but they often focus on more valuable herbs like basil and mint.

00:03:13.420 --> 00:03:19.060
Aquaponics is one of the most
sustainable agricultural systems,

00:03:19.060 --> 00:03:24.340
but it still has a long way to go before becoming a go-to farming method.

00:03:24.980 --> 00:03:33.020
New developments are continuing to make the process more customizable, automated, and efficient than ever.

00:03:33.020 --> 00:03:38.560
Who knows, in the future your salad 
might even be grown by a fish!

00:03:47.020 --> 00:03:51.020
What food would you grow in your
aquaponic system?

00:03:51.020 --> 00:03:52.360
Let us know in the comments below.

